//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="roomy true | breached true | reclaimed true | zero_cap true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.mem.budget, mem.region.cap.1, mem.region.cap.2, mem.region.cap.3, conc.proc.exit
//!
//! The flagship: a region-budget breach reaches the caller as an
//! ORDINARY ERROR ROW. This is D68's whole containment story
//! (`[mem.region.cap.3]`, `[conc.proc.exit]`'s `fault(kind)`) collapsed
//! into one `else` arm — the shape lobo's per-request 503 consumes, and
//! the reason this module exists at all.
//!
//! Four claims, all in one run:
//!
//!  - **A budget that fits is invisible.** `with_cap` with a roomy
//!    budget returns normally and raises nothing.
//!  - **A budget that does not fit is the row `exhausted`**, not a dead
//!    process. The trap fires at the allocating site inside the proc
//!    (`trap(alloc-contract)`, the existing kind — a byte budget is an
//!    allocation contract, `[mem.region.cap.1]`), is CONTAINED at the
//!    proc boundary, and reaches the join as a value.
//!  - **The memory is already back.** `[mem.region.cap.3]` makes
//!    teardown free-then-deliver, and this file measures it from the
//!    OUTSIDE: `live()` after the breach equals `live()` before the
//!    call. A supervisor that admits new work on this row was handed
//!    the memory back first, which is the half of the contract that
//!    makes the row safe to act on.
//!  - **`cap: 0` is a legal budget** (`[mem.region.cap.2]`) that every
//!    charge breaches, so `with_cap(0, …)` over work that allocates is
//!    the honest spelling of "this may allocate nothing".
//!
//! The budgets here are hand-picked rather than derived, and that is
//! safe for exactly the reason the module header warns callers it is
//! usually NOT: the two sides are an order of magnitude apart in every
//! tier's units, not one byte apart. A witness that needed the exact
//! boundary would have to measure it with a dry run first
//! (`[mem.region.cap.1]`'s ledger-units sentence, wolf-lang#203), which
//! is what wolf-lang's own `faults/region_cap_breach.lu` does.
//!
//! LANES: lupin and native. `wolf conform-run --checked` refuses this
//! file — "structured concurrency in checked execution (C1 deferred)" —
//! because D68's containment mechanism IS a proc and the checked
//! executor defers the whole task layer. That refusal is the tier's,
//! not this surface's: no spelling of a contained breach avoids it.

use std.mem.budget

fn tiny() {
    var xs = List[int]()
    for i in 0..8 {
        (mut xs).push(i)
    }
}

fn hefty() {
    var xs = List[int]()
    for i in 0..4000 {
        (mut xs).push(i)
    }
}

fn main() -> !int {
    let base = budget.live()

    var roomy = true
    budget.with_cap(100000000, tiny) else |exhausted| {
        roomy = false
    }

    var breached = false
    budget.with_cap(64, hefty) else |exhausted| {
        breached = true
    }

    let reclaimed = budget.live() == base

    var zero_cap = false
    budget.with_cap(0, tiny) else |exhausted| {
        zero_cap = true
    }

    print("roomy {roomy} | breached {breached} | reclaimed {reclaimed} | zero_cap {zero_cap}")
    if !roomy { return 1 }
    if !breached { return 2 }
    if !reclaimed { return 3 }
    if !zero_cap { return 4 }
    0
}
